#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171751 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3e3fdc8-67d0-5b46-9c6b-c1ec8d4ea24f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Asymptotes"}] :question/id #uuid "8a1be39d-6621-5d71-b1a4-3669d210ee8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6986" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/d59da4d56d2d664641b5b52136db98c70c6d8ca3efb6903cc7524f748776486e.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' = x + y + 1$\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{xy}{y+1}$\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{y}{x+1}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/a692d04d777a7770bfed51d27e53e4d5a0f19860a22afa598875b64bdd85a25c.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x \\to -1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $y=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = x + y + 1$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0$ (the value depends on $x$).\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = \\dfrac{xy}{y+1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0,$ but \n\n$$\ny' = \\dfrac{xy}{y+1} \\to -\\dfrac{y}{y+1} \n\\quad \\textrm{as} \\quad \nx \\to -1,\n$$\n\nand hence $y'$ depends on $y$ along the line $x=-1.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = \\dfrac{y}{x+1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0,$ and \n\n$$\ny' = \\bigg| \\dfrac{y}{x+1} \\bigg| \\to \\infty\n\\quad \\textrm{as} \\quad \nx \\to -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171752 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1616167b-5679-5ff6-b385-3e1b59352c64" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes and Factoring"}] :question/id #uuid "f43aebf0-6aa9-58cf-9475-69f5af74d71f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6988" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/3a0ec9ab00e65eb1b63a6b73c81c615d7388617814ba6351b1575a96b732eb4d.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n- $y' = x^2(y+1)+2(y+1)$\n\n- $y' = x(y+1)+2(y+1)$\n\n- $y' = x(y+1)^2+2(y+1)^2$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/b64005397add7c39b60c49bc708f3ee8cccc2e03786278df22e914fc928998db.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-2$ and $y=-1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-2$ and $y=-1$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y > -1$ | $\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$ | $\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <      -1$ | $\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$ | $\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$ |\n|  | $x< -2$ | $x> -2$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\ny' = x^2(y+1)+2(y+1) = (x^2 + 2)(y + 1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $y = -1$ only.\n\n- Equation II gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\ny' = x(y+1)+2(y+1) = (x+2)(y+1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x = -2$ and $y = -1,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y > -1$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <      -1$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ |\n|  | $x< -2$ | $x> -2$ |\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\ny' = x(y+1)^2+2(y+1)^2 = (x+2)(y+1)^2\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $y=-1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y >    -1$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <   -1$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ |\n|  | $x< -2$ | $x> -2$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is equation II."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171753 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "244419de-9b13-5923-b0a5-1bdc6f9b03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"}] :question/id #uuid "4dabeee5-1c46-5198-9c51-9099fb5df617" :question/math-academy-id "e-7126" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/fc130ae2d3404808ba1cbaa66667979728c535320934bb0dbff463ef467572e4.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' = 2xy$\n\n- $y' = -x(y-1)^2$\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{1}{2}x^2(y-1)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/0c3b7cc8b39735429b1f39e8421fd280e4687f15c5806af0b102900b5e3f8cd8.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=1$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y > 1$ | $\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$ | $\\nearrow$\n\n$({\\color{blue}{+}})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <     1$ | $\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$ | $\\searrow$\n\n$({\\color{red}{-}})$ |\n|  | $x<     0$ | $x> 0$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = 2xy$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = -x(y-1)^2$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y > 1$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <     1$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ |\n|  | $x<     0$ | $x> 0$ |\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of $y' = \\dfrac{1}{2}x^2(y-1)$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=1,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y > 1$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ | ${\\color{blue}{+}}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <     1$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ | ${\\color{red}{-}}$ |\n|  | $x<     0$ | $x> 0$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171820 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd82d96b-34d5-5d20-b810-7ea88b0b5797" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Stationary Points"}] :question/id #uuid "95746e3f-5cdf-58e2-8dbb-39b3cbe5cfc1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9356" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/4adbd41200bb3fdcd75f7f19530ca810cb67970b732e897845bfd621fee180b3.png)\n\n \n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by $y' = (x-a)(y-b).$ Find the value of $a + b.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/5401c18589eeafd8ac4139de98fb41879d03c0731938c42b2dae50da629de0ce.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x = -2$ and $y = 2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\ny' = (x+2)(y-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a = -2,$ $b = 2,$ and $a + b=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "244419de-9b13-5923-b0a5-1bdc6f9b03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75fedbf0-380e-55b5-b710-26a3040ae923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(1-x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325078} {:answer/id #uuid "3d48f14f-21ed-5c11-9450-47228d5b8484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(x+1)^{2}(1-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325079} {:answer/id #uuid "50090f04-bf0c-5dff-b288-221326600712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={x}^{2}(1-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325080} {:answer/id #uuid "2336cfd6-bfd3-567a-b610-f691eaba08ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(x+1)(y-1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325081} {:answer/id #uuid "08b6f557-adcb-5222-a3ba-acadbda18e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(x+1)^{2}(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d48f14f-21ed-5c11-9450-47228d5b8484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(x+1)^{2}(1-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325079} :answer-field/id #uuid "25b34d65-4865-5eb2-bae7-c66e3c4fd8f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2479bfa7-d3b6-56be-a91b-071846ad7ca5" :question/math-academy-id "q-117982" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/fe5c82553a127e5b7690d13114e7f06cae20eaea83e0a4d62b994bb4c64a27d6.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/e463bb775deea7ce2f2840d6c3554fffbb93483c6c8694933e78c3c89cca9711.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(x+1)^{2}(1-y) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(x+1)(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-1$ and $y=1$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>1$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<1$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<-1$ | $x>-1$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x+1)^{2}(1-y).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325083 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1616167b-5679-5ff6-b385-3e1b59352c64" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes and Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "754cd560-c7da-5485-b429-5df20753653b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325085} {:answer/id #uuid "7e45ae5d-cdce-59f0-93c4-8fca8f540255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325086} {:answer/id #uuid "84fdf06f-ba87-5be9-9232-68630117c9d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325087} {:answer/id #uuid "97b41c76-318e-5956-90f8-a34c62cb1bbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325088} {:answer/id #uuid "715679c9-36c4-5556-b41e-d2103ecfcfca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84fdf06f-ba87-5be9-9232-68630117c9d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325087} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6ad0701-6d7b-59a9-8e5d-8bc9bc8f7ea2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32bf006e-92d4-579e-b2bf-3f6592341daa" :question/math-academy-id "q-118025" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/5077a94fe8b5d5435b5bf50d0ea687f5bc87ae95011e22800ccfe473f8af78dd.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=xy-x$\n- ${y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}+2xy+x$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/2f7075161363dee2817aeb8aff9e85781a10bd30c926bfff73724992eee699ac.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=-1$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>-1$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(y+1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy-x=x(y-1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=1.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}+2xy+x=x(y+1)^{2}\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd82d96b-34d5-5d20-b810-7ea88b0b5797" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Stationary Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3422420c-f0ac-501f-8ca0-92d6b04f54db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325092} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ad3ac4-7e44-527f-8c13-8f0a409b5501" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325093} {:answer/id #uuid "0d3aeb28-1026-5c4c-af27-82a30b9d38f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325094} {:answer/id #uuid "9b040570-b10e-577c-8e66-fa8afd2bb31a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325095} {:answer/id #uuid "12ff01fb-ea77-520a-bb84-47a60b44e1b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d3aeb28-1026-5c4c-af27-82a30b9d38f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325094} :answer-field/id #uuid "21579536-e349-501e-8d7a-68b0d8d83928" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d7d9095-27e4-513b-91cc-01e27a6a05fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-120228" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/204be6af825ef678e06526bff1fdac14ca5f09a2a7bee939620f3f2be1496985.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x(3-y)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(x+3)y$\n- ${y}^{′}=(3-x)y$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/9051213882bcb506ddae46e0a6a79c8cd03a10eadd89e0007f895f68b00c33de.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=3$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x(3-y)$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=3.$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=(x+3)y$ evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=(3-x)y$ evaluates to zero at $x=3$ and $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "244419de-9b13-5923-b0a5-1bdc6f9b03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b2a3804-ff13-5b69-95e7-8249d1199f48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325099} {:answer/id #uuid "08268eca-5ee6-56a5-837c-b3abb2cad0e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325100} {:answer/id #uuid "1d32451e-db89-5922-808b-ebd629f5a901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325101} {:answer/id #uuid "c268439d-8b2b-5441-ab09-8a9f6e2bb1dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325102} {:answer/id #uuid "fc04d102-8da8-5c93-a5f7-69d2a8d57fd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08268eca-5ee6-56a5-837c-b3abb2cad0e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325100} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f22b5d1-7a3c-52d0-aeb4-23f2b77a9406" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0743b379-9d8c-5e5c-9d40-afdce6a3665b" :question/math-academy-id "q-120232" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/be758c93cb1e04380de9b57103c302623400342946996030b22ae12046488172.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x(3-y)$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(y-3)$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(y-3)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/3afe29e9dd507cd9aae4035ef3bde2f3f2c1865cd258c4d218ec1934d8626a6e.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=3,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=3$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>3$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<3$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x(3-y)$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=3,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>3$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(y-3)$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=3,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>3$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<3$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x(y-3)^{2}$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=3,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "244419de-9b13-5923-b0a5-1bdc6f9b03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfde55e7-f754-5917-9567-c00ccef5e625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325106} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7078fc-3f15-5c76-bb35-4d850c66eaf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325107} {:answer/id #uuid "c78d937a-5276-5820-9830-0c3023df64b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325108} {:answer/id #uuid "626f051f-b73e-5b05-83e1-46ce978c40ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325109} {:answer/id #uuid "1d44ab19-d16e-54dd-b7a7-b9efddcc7943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/i