vior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=(x-2)^{2}y$ evaluates to zero at $x=2$ and $y=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=(x-2)y$ evaluates to zero at $x=2$ and $y=0,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1616167b-5679-5ff6-b385-3e1b59352c64" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes and Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a30bcebf-555d-557f-9bde-0d61f2b5da88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325113} {:answer/id #uuid "b9114df4-b863-5ba3-a7f7-f342e0a06bbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325114} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa217cb-5fae-57d0-a05a-98b85ad0c22c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325115} {:answer/id #uuid "1b594cad-98e4-53f8-a9da-78baab2e3ae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325116} {:answer/id #uuid "a0eb2493-5368-50cf-b8cf-cbe1d70fddf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0eb2493-5368-50cf-b8cf-cbe1d70fddf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325117} :answer-field/id #uuid "366c97a8-585e-5146-8711-67be543f6a57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1561474-973c-5cfc-a746-63e22b2dd80c" :question/math-academy-id "q-120264" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/f4a6aa53604ce4381a6627097ba4ebc5853a67ff9fa1675b98e5114775cefdc6.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=4-y$\n- ${y}^{′}=8y-2xy$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}y-8xy+16y$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/7d0d7ff6fa9c7cd074fcc2321f125ac5da8142e55578459f44903555e2797204.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=4$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=4$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<4$ | $x>4$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4-y\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $y=4$ only.\n\n- Equation II gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=8y-2xy=2y(4-x)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=4$ and $y=0,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<4$ | $x>4$ |\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y-8xy+16y=(x-4)^{2}y\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=4$ and $y=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<4$ | $x>4$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1616167b-5679-5ff6-b385-3e1b59352c64" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes and Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b761c5c1-0e48-53e7-9446-b0de8e1c72a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325120} {:answer/id #uuid "4b7215a2-f616-5258-a788-12ae74be13d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325121} {:answer/id #uuid "375b6910-72ef-563a-bb36-c4ab02187aec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325122} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c9fbde-5300-5bc2-ab52-0103e71374d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325123} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6650b2-ceea-57fc-ace9-0cac92609c53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "375b6910-72ef-563a-bb36-c4ab02187aec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325122} :answer-field/id #uuid "90ad1467-e76d-5813-afa6-0e80d909a31f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67abfe2c-1281-50f8-b333-ecdd246c2e1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-120266" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/26a0a41cc50b6d5cb586c52e6a4e6bd5bd324f3e13ad7eef4ee0d9eed18340ea.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=2y-xy$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4x{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/249aba327a8103645963ce95e7935019a279b4cf0d9306111c41172ae22f792b.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=2$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=2$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y-xy=(2-x)y\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=2$ and $y=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4x{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=(x+2)^{2}{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=(x-2){y}^{2}\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=2$ and $y=0,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1616167b-5679-5ff6-b385-3e1b59352c64" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes and Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d55e3a56-ca1d-55ee-8bd8-790096326790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325127} {:answer/id #uuid "e31b4755-412b-534e-9829-4e63da2427da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325128} {:answer/id #uuid "1a6ac111-251b-5f3c-9daf-5091db063458" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325129} {:answer/id #uuid "c990d511-bdfc-52f2-9f97-3607803ecf20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325130} {:answer/id #uuid "15eb2826-1108-5b63-8052-4dca02c8e3c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15eb2826-1108-5b63-8052-4dca02c8e3c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325131} :answer-field/id #uuid "73072c98-7204-5ac9-8aae-098750834d7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1351bc74-f935-5fb4-ae5a-9f775594bf2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-120271" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/85fcea0ad26f7da26189c7611a851bfe228d3ad5d428dcba6c8299c8304540d1.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=(x-1){y}^{2}-x+1$\n- ${y}^{′}=xy-y-(x-1)$\n- ${y}^{′}=y-1-x(y-1)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/dc75845932358d060d5878a30f00fa01e2b7b54408bca308110a53ea654ae18c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=1$ and $y=1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=1$ and $y=1$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>1$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<1$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x-1){y}^{2}-x+1=(x-1)(y+1)(y-1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=1$ and $y=\\pm 1.$\n\n- Equation II gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy-y-(x-1)=(x-1)(y-1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=1$ and $y=1,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<1$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y-1-x(y-1)=(1-x)(y-1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=1$ and $y=1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>1$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3e3fdc8-67d0-5b46-9c6b-c1ec8d4ea24f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Asymptotes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ea62f2f-4926-5fd1-a6dc-043fffc13980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170918} {:answer/id #uuid "90b5cd5a-2b22-5c46-8f94-67c263f1329c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = \\frac{1}{1 + x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170919} {:answer/id #uuid "ef868f3c-79e8-589d-a876-a74c23a8c4b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = \\sin (x + y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170920} {:answer/id #uuid "2fbcaab5-96a7-5e06-9994-bb3d87f0d2b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = \\frac{y}{1 + x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170921} {:answer/id #uuid "356f31ad-99dc-5f76-a79f-fcc60474b0e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = y - x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90b5cd5a-2b22-5c46-8f94-67c263f1329c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = \\frac{1}{1 + x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170919} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eda099f-580a-595e-991a-58faa4af3438" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1eda5ee0-9c5c-5074-a654-a6372da78ad5" :question/math-academy-id "q-49940" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/423d393e3ec1f666f384da26ce1df67fd38b977ccad6ff1f56360346ed36d0cc.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/423d393e3ec1f666f384da26ce1df67fd38b977ccad6ff1f56360346ed36d0cc.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope lines on the same vertical line are all the same. This means that the slope does not vary with $y.$ In other words, ${y}^{′}$ is a function of $x$ only.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & {y}^{′}={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$ \n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}$ become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\nBy contrast, \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd82d96b-34d5-5d20-b810-7ea88b0b5797" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Stationary Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50802887-5047-500b-a9ca-d0197f348f64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325134} {:answer/id #uuid "bc5b028f-4b3c-599f-b98f-6380eae44dc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325135} {:answer/id #uuid "2212574b-ff09-5f21-a4fc-2d66312d56b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325136} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9629c7-1090-5ec2-b38f-9b0501eb21d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325137} {:answer/id #uuid "6258b48d-092c-5b79-8eb6-49fcb9ee5097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6258b48d-092c-5b79-8eb6-49fcb9ee5097" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325138} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e9d9804-b664-5b69-a841-fc0826e9828b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48261767-3c69-5169-b7b8-70bb8d04f5b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-50107" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/bd48f9adcb39961a1ea30314566857bcfb92889b313453bfe76a1aa5405a9e7e.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by ${y}^{′}=(x-a)(y-b).$ Find the value of $b-a.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/a7df5da44a77085b7a89685aa80220a8ae4879cb55c350dba294a5b2e281cac9.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-1$ and $y=-2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x+1)(y+2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-1,$ $b=-2,$ and $b-a=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd82d96b-34d5-5d20-b810-7ea88b0b5797" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Stationary Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1f7b36e-81b6-5f26-9623-ea06a47ecbb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170930} {:answer/id #uuid "eb42cf7c-c7db-5cd0-9626-746a1549b06c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170931} {:answer/id #uuid "fe271817-310b-5dd6-b4de-f1f1ab74f225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170932} {:answer/id #uuid "05aaa79c-9e1f-5f47-b280-a36ff852b08c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170933} {:answer/id #uuid "678ddbbe-f5a9-52b9-b37c-b6cdc5572abe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1f7b36e-81b6-5f26-9623-ea06a47ecbb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170930} :answer-field/id #uuid "aed9eade-2553-502d-9ecc-c13188eb31c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41ff9457-ceff-56ea-afc9-4b6f116bc651" :question/math-academy-id "q-50108" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/fb373420ddaa81aa41e85145dce11d71068740e8039522c5d68d861de606bec3.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by\n${y}^{′}=(x-a)(y-b).$\nFind the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/e9011b88061abae826e12742ccd47a272bef9e969f204cd7e8ca63edf908a7f8.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=2$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x-2)(y-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=2,$ $b=2,$ and $a+b=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd82d96b-34d5-5d20-b810-7ea88b0b5797" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Stationary Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4aea5516-9024-51d8-ae06-44f4757411ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325141} {:answer/id #uuid "c24690fc-cfcd-5492-a7bc-9384318369df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325142} {:answer/id #uuid "17fa48c6-de1f-5d6c-90ea-eac9f19064f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325143} {:answer/id #uuid "67f4dccb-e141-5cec-be92-14bdc51dfccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325144} {:answer/id #uuid "7a463d75-1ec4-5c75-8ef1-65cc9c0a1848" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4aea5516-9024-51d8-ae06-44f4757411ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325141} :answer-field/id #uuid "d89c2edf-2b16-5c3f-9e26-06054752eb7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d81ab6b-bf13-5007-90e1-5f3de11a9199" :question/math-academy-id "q-50110" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/d714de862ed316ead00880607077c9090c54cc7c63adc849b5e83190118afec2.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by ${y}^{′}=(x-a)(y-b).$ Find the value of $2a-b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the slope field, we see that the slope is zero whenever $x=-3$ or $y=3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/10ae8ed1fe49b32938dba062ad13e39ce1339c6ac9b94d67f679ab60b288be00.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-3$ and $y=3,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x+3)(y-3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-3,$ $b=3,$ and $2a-b=2(-3)-3=-9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd82d96b-34d5-5d20-b810-7ea88b0b5797" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Stationary Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6955f7f-c502-5145-9a31-37d9fb407848" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325148} {:answer/id #uuid "98674d0b-e981-5b2f-a7b5-2d8de6341cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325149} {:answer/id #uuid "0647e010-a9c1-53a7-a741-5db6a0f04cee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325150} {:answer/id #uuid "ec7a165a-22d1-5a24-9fd5-be65a7b1d524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325151} {:answer/id #uuid "8035c635-438e-5b40-8a4b-c504b6bd5df8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec7a165a-22d1-5a24-9fd5-be65a7b1d524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325151} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7ddd512-d9c5-5c56-b265-e760316dd66d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e09007b1-58b7-53da-9003-c7cf000369f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-50143" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/50472b92dc5fbcc8688bb1d5459f910fb2d3f3c0ee564ba671cb6a53ce8df721.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}={e}^{y+1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(y+1)$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/e328914d55b82fdd07147f683aa44aebbc3f3e908f180e6632332119c81ce482.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the line $y=-1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on this line. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$ is never zero.\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}={e}^{y+1}$ is never zero.\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x(y+1)$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "244419de-9b13-5923-b0a5-1bdc6f9b03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abb14f53-6bec-53fe-a24d-45f6d2ce62ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = (x + 1)(y - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170936} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8ac526-70a8-54a5-8643-b3b4014bedbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = (x - 1)(y - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170937} {:answer/id #uuid "10b4bbd6-4311-50c9-9586-ad4d315a4aa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = (x - 2)(y - 2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170938} {:answer/id #uuid "4add3a1a-bad8-5da6-835d-bd2e4c13ef4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = (x - 2)(y + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170939} {:answer/id #uuid "643a7854-0193-5241-9111-336c307c71af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = (x - 2)(y - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10b4bbd6-4311-50c9-9586-ad4d315a4aa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{′} = (x - 2)(y - 2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170938} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f7440b5-30c6-570c-9ad5-1aa323e029ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4cb5a8df-a5e4-5c99-96b2-ca3bba93068e" :question/math-academy-id "q-50160" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/50e6b2cca3b08d4b98d1a7be174816cd8e3d726b4e9af29e6605bf365a474eb3.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/0b09389629bdd17043a57b2e39551543475e2d19772b41d7b74d3293f4c91aa6.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=2$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(x-2)(y-2)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(x-2)(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=2$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>2$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<2$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x-2)(y-2)^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1616167b-5679-5ff6-b385-3e1b59352c64" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes and Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4af591a6-64ca-56e7-b711-a5eade68d3fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=xy+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325155} {:answer/id #uuid "e7107c47-1d14-5e01-8f2a-8e5233313fa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(x+1)^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325156} {:answer/id #uuid "58b1714f-106f-5ced-9122-0296a4071c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=(x+1)(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325157} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc1eeff-b05d-553c-951b-500caf54fed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325158} {:answer/id #uuid "de32585f-ebcc-5dcc-a67d-09777688bae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4af591a6-64ca-56e7-b711-a5eade68d3fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=xy+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325155} :answer-field/id #uuid "62ddb129-9349-5841-b7ff-98b4d259055e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b479fc1-a9fe-5521-9397-979fdf022a74" :question/math-academy-id "q-50187" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/ac08ec40d4aeae745d9aafd2696fc5da7977aa9d01c2766798ac546b829cb112.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/2e9bd658eb426a7e2d5869f2fd9499e846ab82f25edcef6dc5c6f42e38b188bd.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-1$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy+y=(x+1)y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(x+1)^{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-1$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n|  | $x<-1$ | $x>-1$ |\n\n \nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy+y=(x+1)y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=xy+y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "244419de-9b13-5923-b0a5-1bdc6f9b03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93ab0417-4704-568b-9373-31611d88f941" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170948} {:answer/id #uuid "b5fc65ef-8502-5176-a4aa-5eee83d077fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170949} {:answer/id #uuid "cf968c17-0048-51a7-9509-52e1324dd379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170950} {:answer/id #uuid "2e2dc7d0-b315-514a-9e91-15259c0eff56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170951} {:answer/id #uuid "2e57cd97-0e91-58fc-83ed-b4c1f4706dd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5fc65ef-8502-5176-a4aa-5eee83d077fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170949} :answer-field/id #uuid "143b59ad-abc0-5890-b0af-ce3472ede21d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc5e6ef1-2f84-542e-97fb-884aded42acc" :question/math-academy-id "q-50189" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/35f52e6a2c3eafe59fea3c14351067fdf4b78b8c2f247454429952eb3a6f8270.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=2xy$\n- ${y}^{′}=-xy$\n- ${y}^{′}=x+1$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/e3afa2080ea81f679c9c9b3423cfbe7b11bed5625854a0a33ff01312ca3629e4.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=2xy$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n- Equation II gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-xy$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=0,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x+1$ evaluates to zero at $x=-1$ only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3e3fdc8-67d0-5b46-9c6b-c1ec8d4ea24f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Asymptotes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c62a4f3-3c40-546c-a3e4-c2e2537cab7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{x+y}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325162} {:answer/id #uuid "8225c449-bbbc-5fb0-99ab-39a1226cbea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325163} {:answer/id #uuid "73c44374-dbf3-5791-a31c-6448018eeb91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{2x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325164} {:answer/id #uuid "09bae4de-8ce8-5d4a-a6bb-b1833f460385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325165} {:answer/id #uuid "f8aa5643-1e40-5829-9d50-92df816e118e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09bae4de-8ce8-5d4a-a6bb-b1833f460385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325165} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecb4e7c3-58c2-518b-9f13-a0089b40a921" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ac42386b-8b3d-519d-b863-14e7e83b8ab6" :question/math-academy-id "q-50347" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/6f3fa328fce3347548661ec19a4a7bcd628df59d603885445c215bcf7e23eee5.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/78f281b8039a5897ca8013324c676095b6e1f57278cbab35b05c5eb3b4360301.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes become horizontal along the lines $y=\\pm x.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {y}^{′}=\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & {y}^{′}={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, for $x\\ne 0,$ the slopes become vertical as $y\\to 0.$ \n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ne 0,$ notice that\n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{{y}^{2}}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{{y}^{2}}$ become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\nBy contrast, \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\to -{x}^{2}\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{{y}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325167 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3e3fdc8-67d0-5b46-9c6b-c1ec8d4ea24f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Asymptotes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0932323-5bc1-5fdd-890c-1a4e840a0f9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{x-y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325169} {:answer/id #uuid "e3654ca6-5b17-546e-ab38-eaf99d3c034e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{x+y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325170} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9f0b36-5b23-503c-b219-88f26372aa1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325171} {:answer/id #uuid "79383d78-369c-504a-9965-dd8156c9e240" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325172} {:answer/id #uuid "81458264-689e-5e61-a3fd-1bdae15f7f9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3654ca6-5b17-546e-ab38-eaf99d3c034e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{′}=\\frac{x+y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325170} :answer-field/id #uuid "9608b28e-267b-5c91-b8cf-d9bf68620849" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bb7cc510-cda6-5038-820e-790968629172" :question/math-academy-id "q-50348" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/097a662a461b31dbcadc4e3fc2d9cb5c439c251792b72c768581055783e35379.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/9cee91c4500060ff7b8cb2e35a8748614b9bb7a9b267e2c8ee58d6273a7561f1.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes become horizontal along the lines $y=-x.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {y}^{′}=\\frac{x+y}{x} \\\\  & {y}^{′}=x+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, for $y\\ne 0,$ the slopes become vertical as $x\\to 0.$ \n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{x+y}{x}.\n$$\n\nFor $y\\ne 0,$ notice that\n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{x+y}{x}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x+y}{x}$ become vertical as $x\\to 0.$\nBy contrast, \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+y\\to y\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{x+y}{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3e3fdc8-67d0-5b46-9c6b-c1ec8d4ea24f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Asymptotes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24f19954-91d9-5839-8dfd-4430481d7315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325176} {:answer/id #uuid "025ac7b8-4a9e-5c70-9993-e44c5128e455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325177} {:answer/id #uuid "cb483467-da99-555e-b04b-2627b9c53591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325178} {:answer/id #uuid "0a46a023-3a2f-5832-9f01-1afe62ba4634" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325179} {:answer/id #uuid "0d3bb27d-769c-550d-8d71-911fcefe5bc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb483467-da99-555e-b04b-2627b9c53591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325178} :answer-field/id #uuid "1079a8c8-1903-584d-927e-27e2e9a1c3c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bb29a817-c88f-55f6-8e13-5c432b07e294" :question/math-academy-id "q-50349" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/6a64064f114ac2e6acafa791c4fda50849cad6f3387c4793bdf991c95ae8e542.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y+1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=x+y$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y-1}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/6a119b24c773a25bf3530dbfb8574c710cc37a5e970d92112aee5f489551ef02.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $x=0.$\n\n- For $x\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to -1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y+1}$ evaluates to zero on $x=0,$ and for $x\\ne 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{x}{y+1}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to -1.\n$$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x+y$ does not evaluate to zero on $x=0$ (the value depends on $y$).\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y-1}$ evaluates to zero on $x=0,$ but for $x\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=|\\frac{x}{y-1}|\\to \\frac{|x|}{2}\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3e3fdc8-67d0-5b46-9c6b-c1ec8d4ea24f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Asymptotes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf97dfe7-ea37-541e-bb24-c56fc6f92f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325183} {:answer/id #uuid "b7af04da-b588-555f-bb33-9a3ae36b30b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325184} {:answer/id #uuid "ec314ef5-0543-5793-880c-30055750aaf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325185} {:answer/id #uuid "363fe3be-c5ea-5733-982a-1ac18e6b1ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325186} {:answer/id #uuid "caf3ac79-6375-5ed5-9bf6-dd9c6efd221c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec314ef5-0543-5793-880c-30055750aaf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325185} :answer-field/id #uuid "935c9102-fd8f-517d-8075-a759fa9ddcc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30bd4061-472a-5533-b81a-a42df0c3f79b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80714" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/fd7e9afcccbbb635d6bde139697aec0cbaa4bad486ea8b098e96f74bfbefebc9.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{y-1}$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/28c4751694caf50f08f73d7ccf3525877e6679901d49854951810f550a65ede2.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope lines on the same horizontal line are all the same. This means that the slope does not vary with $x.$ In other words, ${y}^{′}$ is a function of $y$ only.\n\n- Second, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$ is a function of $y$ only, but\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 1.\n$$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only.\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{y-1}$ is a function of $y$ only, and \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{2}{y-1}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}]}
